WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.190
One of the things that I like about National
Review, and always liked about National Review,

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:07.660
is that we weren't just there to go after
the Left, which we do.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.420
We were also there to police our own side.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.730
And I've tried to keep that up.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.380
Hi, I'm Nick Gillespie with Reason TV, and
we're talking with Charles W. Cooke.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:23.650
He is the newly appointed editor of National
Review Online, and the author of The Conservatarian

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:24.749
Manifesto.

00:00:24.749 --> 00:00:25.749
Thanks for talking to us, Charles.

00:00:25.749 --> 00:00:26.749
Thank you for having me.

00:00:26.749 --> 00:00:30.960
You are also originally from Britain until
you migrated this way.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.930
What's your take on Brexit?

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:33.930
I was pro.

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:35.200
I was pleased.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.200
I was in England.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:37.960
I didn't expect it to happen.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:43.650
I'd hoped it would happen... for probably
20 years, long before I was political, actually.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:49.520
Is there anything good about the EU that gets
lost if England moves out of it?

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:50.520
That's the debate.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.230
So, I'm going to tell you first why I was
in favor of it, and then we can go on to that,

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.220
because that's a really important point.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:59.950
I've been in favor of Britain's leaving the
EU for a long time because I'm in favor of

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:00.950
sovereignty.

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:05.119
To me, if you are going to have a parliament,
you should be bound by that parliament.

00:01:05.119 --> 00:01:07.679
On this, I was with the British socialist
Tony Benn.

00:01:07.679 --> 00:01:10.729
"You have got to be able to defeat the people
who govern you."

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:12.900
Probably the only thing I ever agreed with
him on.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:18.999
He said it would be theft of public rights
to say somebody, "Vote for me, and I will

00:01:18.999 --> 00:01:23.030
look after your interests... oh, actually,
I've loaned those powers elsewhere."

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:31.319
Now, there seems to have been a small majority
in Britain in favor of that proposition.

00:01:31.319 --> 00:01:35.920
But there's now going to be an almighty fight
as to what the parliament does with that power

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:37.280
when it's repatriated.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.170
But I think, for many people who voted for
it, this was a question of outcomes.

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:45.140
They thought, "Well, if we leave the EU, we
get rid of free migration.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:48.889
If we leave the EU, we get rid of the free
trade agreements.

00:01:48.889 --> 00:01:52.189
If we leave the EU, we get rid of this regulation
or that regulation."

00:01:52.189 --> 00:01:53.689
Now that's not necessarily the case.

00:01:53.689 --> 00:01:59.970
One of the things that people were trying to
do is to take the energy and the animus and

00:01:59.979 --> 00:02:04.329
anger behind Brexit and say, "Ok, this is
parallel to something going on with Trump."

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:08.110
National Review back in December, of course,
came out with a Never Trump issue.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:12.550
Is there a meaningful parallel between these
events, or the energy, and, if it's good to

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:18.580
Brexit in England, does that mean that Trump
should be given a second look in America?

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:19.420
On the latter question...

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:20.860
no.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:22.730
There is a parallel, but there's also a difference.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:27.970
So, the parallel is that some of the people
who voted for Brexit are annoyed with what

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:31.000
they think of as negative globalization.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.330
They're annoyed with immigration.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.460
They're annoyed with free trade.

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:37.800
They think that Britain is being sold out
to this invisible cabal.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.960
And I think you see that within the Trump
movement.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:44.700
Where it's different is there's also a lot
of, if you will, Never Trumpers who would've

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:51.000
been pro-Brexit, because they would've believed
in old-fashioned ideas like sovereignty, of,

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.280
sort of, constitutional integrity.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.310
I don't think, personally, that it helps Trump.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:03:00.380
First of all, Britain is a different country,
has a different culture.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:04.010
It's a different question, "Do you wish to
change the way your country relates with the

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:09.780
world in terms of its fundamental sovereignty"
from, "Do you wish this person to be president?"

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.470
And, I think, what we're seeing thus far,
at least, is that people don't want Trump

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:14.650
to be their president.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:20.360
In your stimulating and popular book, The
Conservatarian Manifesto, who are the conservatarians

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:23.500
going to vote for in election 2016?

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.580
Well, that's a fascinating question.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:30.320
I think the Republican Party essentially read
my book, insofar as it did at all, and said,

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:31.950
"Let's not do this."

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.210
So, anyone who liked the book-- -

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:35.880
-I think
you're giving them to much credit for reading--

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:39.690
But anyone who liked the book probably is
not voting for Trump.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:41.670
I think that Gary Johnson is popular.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:44.890
But I also think there are a lot of people,
at least my email inbox would suggest, there

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:49.870
are a lot of people who read my book who want
to reclaim the Republican Party but certainly

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:51.270
can't do so until November.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:56.650
You were recently named the editor of National
Review Online, which was really one of the

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:01.280
first, and still one of the best, certainly
most trafficked, but also most provocative

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:03.250
websites for a political magazine...

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:06.680
What direction do you see the site moving
under your editorship?

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:11.450
And what are areas, new areas or new writers,
new things that you're looking to do?

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:15.120
Well, I think we have a pretty good offering
as it is.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.630
Certainly, opposition against Trump is unlikely
to change.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:21.829
One of the things that I like about National
Review, and always have liked about National

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:27.070
Review is that we weren't just there to go
after the Left, which we do.

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:29.940
But we were also there to police our own side.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:31.450
And I've tried to keep that up.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:37.480
I think it's extremely important to call out
people who are nominally with you, whether

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:42.150
it's because they're crony capitalists, whether
it's because they are crooked, or whether

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:48.290
it's because they are in hoc to somebody I
think, in Donald Trump, is a charlatan.

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:49.320
I think that's important.

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:52.120
Also, I have a slightly more libertarian bent.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:54.320
So, that'll probably shine through a little
bit.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.850
National Review has always been a magazine
and a website that has included a number of

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:00.850
viewpoints.

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:02.180
I hope that will continue.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:06.600
But the editor probably has some stamp on
it, so it should move slightly more in the

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:07.639
libertarian direction.

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:10.480
But I can't imagine that it will change too
much, because I think it was pretty good in

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:11.480
the first place.

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:12.480
All right, well, thank you.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:13.480
We'll leave it there.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:14.480
Thank you.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.510
We have been talking with Charles W. Cooke
of National Review.

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:21.630
He's the new editor of National Review Online
and the author of The Conservatarian Manifesto.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:22.630
Thanks so much for talking.

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:23.830
Yeah, thank you for having me.

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:25.470
For Reason TV, I'm Nick Gillespie.

